## 5. BRAND KNOWLEDGE: TURN DOCUMENTS INTO APPROVED RULES

The Brand Knowledge page is a central feature, not just a table of manually entered rules.

### 5.1 Create a usable onboarding flow

When a user has no brand configured, show:

“Create your brand workspace”

Allow the user to enter:
- Brand or organisation name.
- Optional description and industry.
- Optional preferred language.
- Optional website or internal reference information.

After creation, show a clear next step to upload brand knowledge.

Do not insert any default brand or default approved rules.

### 5.2 Support real document uploads

Allow users to upload multiple files in one batch.

For the prototype, support the following where technically feasible in the existing stack:

- PDF, including long policy documents.
- DOCX.
- TXT and Markdown.
- CSV and XLSX for product facts, pricing, terminology and structured tables.
- PNG, JPEG and WebP for brand reference images, approved advertising examples, logos, and other visual assets.

Validate the actual file type, MIME type, size, and upload permissions on the server. Set sensible configurable limits. Explain unsupported formats clearly instead of pretending they were processed.

Provide a proper upload interface with:
- Drag-and-drop and browse.
- File names, types and sizes.
- Upload and processing status.
- Success and failure states per file.
- Retry or remove actions.
- A source library containing previously uploaded materials.
- Processing progress and a clear indication of what still needs attention.

Do not require users to manually retype a 100-page brand guideline.

### 5.3 Build a document ingestion pipeline

For every uploaded source, run a real extraction pipeline.

**PDFs:** Extract native text, page numbers, headings, paragraphs, tables and relevant structural information where possible. Use OCR or the document-capable AI model for scanned PDFs and image-based pages. Preserve page references.

**DOCX:** Extract paragraphs, headings, lists and tables. Preserve section titles.

**TXT/Markdown:** Extract the actual text and meaningful section structure.

**CSV/XLSX:** Extract sheet names, column headers, rows and values. Treat tables such as price lists as structured facts, not just unlabelled text.

**Images:** Use a vision-capable model or a real OCR/vision pipeline to read visible text and identify relevant visual references. Mark unreadable text or uncertain interpretations.

For every source, record:
- Source ID and original filename.
- Organisation and brand IDs.
- MIME type and file size.
- Upload time and processing status.
- Extraction status.
- Page, section, sheet or image references where available.
- Any extraction warnings.

Store original source files in application-managed storage suitable for this prototype. Do not depend on a temporary AI-provider file as the permanent copy.

For long documents, process the content in sections or chunks with overlap where appropriate. Preserve source metadata throughout the process. Do not silently truncate a large document and then report that the entire document was understood.

If a document cannot be processed fully, show exactly which portion or pages were processed and which failed.

### 5.4 AI must convert the extracted material into individual rule candidates

After extraction, analyse the content and identify potential brand-governance rules.

The AI should identify, where supported by the source:

- Official brand names and spelling.
- Product and service names.
- Approved product facts and specifications.
- Prices, plans, dates and other changing facts.
- Approved claims and claims requiring evidence.
- Prohibited, restricted or legally sensitive claims.
- Required and forbidden terminology.
- Tone of voice and writing style.
- Messaging principles and positioning.
- Target audience and communication requirements.
- Calls to action.
- Required disclaimers.
- Formatting and typography requirements.
- Approved visual assets, logos and brand colours.
- Accessibility expectations.
- Channel-specific rules, such as rules for advertisements or social media.
- Approval and publication requirements.

Do not force every extracted passage into a rule. Some passages are background information, examples, explanations or ambiguous statements.

Classify each extracted item as one of:
- Potential rule.
- Approved fact candidate.
- Approved asset candidate.
- Example/reference material.
- Informational context.
- Ambiguous or conflicting instruction.

Every potential rule should have a useful, individual record rather than one enormous combined summary.

### 5.5 Use a proper rule structure

Each rule should support fields similar to:

- Rule ID.
- Brand ID and organisation ID.
- Rule title.
- Rule type/category.
- Plain-language rule statement.
- Trigger, forbidden or matching patterns, when relevant.
- Approved wording, fact or value, when relevant.
- Severity or priority, when justified by the source.
- Source document ID.
- Exact supporting excerpt.
- Page, section, sheet or source location.
- Extraction confidence or uncertainty.
- Approval status.
- Created/updated timestamps.
- Approver and approval timestamp.
- Rule version and source version, when available.

Generate stable identifiers for saved rules. Do not invent a source citation or page number that the extraction pipeline cannot verify.

### 5.6 Implement a candidate review and approval interface

After document processing, show a rule review queue.

The user must be able to:
- Preview the original source.
- Read the extracted rule.
- See its category and relevant approved value.
- Read the supporting source excerpt.
- Open the original page, section or table where possible.
- Edit the title, category, rule text and approved value.
- Approve a rule.
- Reject a rule.
- Keep a rule as a draft.
- Bulk-approve selected candidates after reviewing them.
- Search, filter and sort candidates.
- Review duplicate or conflicting candidates.
- Reprocess a source when extraction fails or needs improvement.

Use the existing Brand Knowledge table layout where possible. The rule list should be comprehensive and searchable, with columns for rule ID/type, rule title and statement, trigger/forbidden patterns, approved content/value, supporting source reference, approval status and actions.

Long rulebooks must be able to produce a long list of separate rules without breaking the UI.

Newly extracted rules must start as candidates or drafts. They must NOT automatically become authoritative approved rules.

If two source documents conflict, identify the conflict, display both source excerpts and ask an authorised human to resolve it. Do not silently select one.

A user should be able to upload an updated policy and review changed, added or potentially outdated rules without destroying the historical record of earlier approved rules.

## 6. IMPLEMENT THE ACTUAL AI INTEGRATION

Use the AI provider and framework already supported by the existing project if an actual working integration exists.

If no suitable model integration exists, use the Gemini API as the first implementation option because the project needs text understanding, document processing and image analysis.

Inspect the current application architecture before choosing the implementation. Do not rewrite the entire project just to introduce another framework.

### 6.1 Secure integration

Use the official, currently supported SDK/API for the selected model.

Keep the model provider configurable through server-side environment variables or the platform's secure secrets manager, for example:

- GEMINI_API_KEY
- GEMINI_MODEL

Use a currently supported, multimodal-capable model for image and PDF analysis. Make the model configurable instead of scattering a model name throughout the source code.

Critical security rules:
- Never hardcode the API key in the frontend.
- Never expose it in browser JavaScript or public configuration.
- Never send it to the user as part of a response.
- Never commit it to source control.
- Make real server-side requests through the backend or a secure server-side API route.
- Display a clear configuration error if the key is missing.
- Handle timeouts, rate limits, unavailable models, malformed responses and API errors.

Provide clear setup instructions for adding the API key to the development environment and the deployment platform's secret settings.

### 6.2 Use structured AI responses

Do not build the core application around unstructured paragraphs produced by a chatbot.

Use schema-constrained JSON responses when supported by the selected provider, then validate the response using application-side schemas and business rules.

Define structured schemas for:
1. Document extraction results.
2. Candidate rule extraction.
3. Rule conflicts and duplicates.
4. Content extraction.
5. Review findings.
6. Suggested corrections.
7. Uncertainty and verification requests.

Validate every response before saving or displaying it. If the model returns invalid or incomplete data, show a recoverable error or retry with safe limits.

### 6.3 Separate deterministic checks from AI judgement

Use deterministic code for checks that can be verified reliably:
- Exact or normalised product-name violations.
- Known forbidden phrases.
- Exact approved prices and numerical facts.
- Dates and specifications where a reliable approved value exists.
- Required disclosures.
- Other explicit rules with testable conditions.

Use AI for contextual checks:
- Tone and voice.
- Messaging consistency.
- Meaning-based claim interpretation.
- Context-sensitive terminology.
- Relevance of a disclaimer.
- Contextual relationship between content and approved rules.
- Visual interpretation where the model can inspect the actual image.

Do not let AI override an authoritative, approved fact without explaining the conflict.

When a deterministic check and a contextual model disagree, preserve the evidence and mark the finding for human review rather than hiding the disagreement.

## 7. BUILD RULE RETRIEVAL: THE CONTENT REVIEW MUST USE APPROVED BRAND KNOWLEDGE

Do not send an entire organisation's library into every AI request.

Implement a retrieval process that:

1. Identifies the organisation and selected brand.
2. Loads only that brand's currently approved and applicable rules.
3. Searches relevant approved rules using categories, terms and reliable matching.
4. Retrieves the corresponding source excerpts and approved values.
5. Sends the submitted content plus the relevant rules and evidence to the analysis model.
6. Validates findings against saved rule IDs and source records.
7. Stores the results with the rule versions that were used.

For the prototype, a hybrid approach is acceptable:
- Deterministic keyword and phrase matching.
- Category and metadata filtering.
- Semantic retrieval or embeddings if supported by the existing architecture and feasible to implement reliably.

If semantic retrieval is implemented, always enforce organisation and brand filters before using retrieved results. Similarity alone does not make a rule applicable.

The review engine must query the approved rules that the user has actually created. It must never silently fall back to NovaPay rules, built-in fictional policies, a model's generic brand assumptions or another customer's documents.

When a brand has no approved rules, explain that a meaningful brand-specific review cannot yet be completed. Offer the user a clear route to process and approve brand knowledge.

