Make Review Content the central working area.

Provide three obvious input methods:
- Paste or type text.
- Upload a document.
- Upload an advertisement or content image.

Support PDF, image and text review as complete, tested workflows. Support other formats only when their extraction and review pipelines are actually implemented.

A creator must be able to submit:
- Advertisement copy.
- Social media captions and posts.
- Product descriptions.
- Marketing emails.
- Campaign briefs.
- PDF documents.
- Advertisement images containing text and visual elements.
- Other supported content formats.

Allow the creator to identify the content type or channel, where relevant. This helps apply appropriate approved rules.

### 8.1 Text submission

Analyse the actual user-provided text. Do not substitute a sample paragraph or precomputed result.

Run applicable deterministic checks, retrieve relevant approved rules and use the model for contextual analysis.

### 8.2 PDF submission

Extract the PDF's real content and preserve page-level references. Analyse relevant text and visual content where the chosen pipeline supports it.

For a long PDF, do not pretend that pages omitted due to a processing limit were checked. Show processing coverage and warnings.

### 8.3 Advertisement image submission

Upload and inspect the actual image bytes. Do not use only a filename, upload metadata, alt text or a description of the image.

Run OCR or vision-based extraction on the image and identify:
- Visible advertising copy.
- Brand and product names.
- Prices, promotional claims and numerical facts.
- Calls to action and disclaimers.
- Key visual elements and layout.
- Logo or reference-asset comparisons where supported.
- Obvious contrast, legibility and accessibility concerns.

Compare readable image text with the selected brand's approved terminology, facts, pricing, claims and disclaimers.

Compare colours, logos and layouts only against actual reference assets and known requirements. Do not claim exact colour matching, pixel-level logo verification or exhaustive accessibility compliance without an appropriate implemented measurement or comparison method.

Where the OCR is uncertain, text is clipped or visual interpretation is unreliable, show “Needs verification” and explain why.

Do not claim an image is fully compliant merely because the visible text did not produce any findings.

### 8.4 Extraction status

Every submission should display one of these meaningful states:
- Uploaded.
- Extracting.
- Ready for review.
- Partially processed.
- Processing failed.

Do not show “Review complete” when the main extraction or analysis request failed.

If extraction is partial, clearly show which parts were checked and which were not.

## 9. ANALYSIS ENGINE: FIND DEFECTS AND EXPLAIN THEM

Run the actual submitted content against the relevant approved rules, with both deterministic checks and AI-assisted contextual analysis.

Use a finding structure similar to:

- Finding ID.
- Review ID.
- Issue category.
- Severity.
- Short title.
- Exact affected text or element, if known.
- Location or page in the submitted content, if available.
- Explanation.
- Applicable approved rule ID.
- Rule version.
- Supporting source document and excerpt.
- Evidence from the submitted content.
- Suggested correction.
- Explanation of how the correction addresses the issue.
- Confidence or uncertainty indicator.
- Finding status.
- Reviewer decision.
- Reviewer comment.

Support issue categories such as:
- Terminology and spelling.
- Approved facts and product information.
- Pricing and numerical inconsistencies.
- Unsupported promotional claims.
- Forbidden or restricted claims.
- Tone and voice.
- Messaging and positioning.
- Required disclaimers.
- Visual brand consistency.
- Accessibility concerns.
- Outdated or conflicting information.
- Human verification required.

Distinguish these situations carefully:

**Confirmed rule violation:** The content conflicts with a specific, approved rule, and the evidence supports that conclusion.

**Approved-fact mismatch:** The content contradicts a known value in an approved source.

**Unsupported claim:** The claim could not be substantiated using the available approved sources. Do not automatically label it false.

**Style suggestion:** A possible improvement rather than a confirmed violation.

**Needs human verification:** Evidence is insufficient, ambiguous, partially extracted or conflicting.

A model must not invent rule IDs, fake source excerpts, fictional approval status, fabricated prices or nonexistent document references.

Every rule-based finding should reference a rule that actually exists in the selected brand's database. If no applicable rule exists, the finding must say so and be classified appropriately.

Use plain language. The creator must understand:
- What is wrong.
- Where the problem appears.
- Which approved rule or fact is relevant.
- Why the current content conflicts with it.
- How the issue can be corrected.
- Whether the result is certain or requires a human decision.

## 10. SUGGEST CORRECTIONS WITHOUT DESTROYING THE CREATOR'S INTENT

Suggestions must correct the identified defect while preserving the original message, audience, purpose and appropriate style.

When possible, display:
- Original wording.
- Suggested wording.
- Explanation.
- Supporting approved source.

The creator must be able to accept, edit or reject each suggestion.

Also provide an option to create a revised draft using selected accepted changes. The revision must be saved separately from the original submitted content.

Never silently overwrite the original content or original advertisement image.

Do not claim to have edited or regenerated the pixels of an uploaded advertisement unless a real image-editing function was actually executed. Copy suggestions and visual-edit instructions must be labelled accurately.

After changes, let the creator re-run the review against the relevant approved rule versions.

## 11. HUMAN REVIEW, COMMENTS AND APPROVAL WORKFLOW

Implement a real workflow with statuses appropriate to the existing product, such as:
- Draft.
- Submitted for review.
- Under review.
- Changes requested.
- Revised.
- Approved.
- Rejected.
- Needs verification.

Authorised users must be able to:
- View findings.
- Add comments.
