## 13. RECURRING ISSUES AND UNCLEAR GUIDELINES

Remove the fake “5 alerts” and any hardcoded recurring-issue chart or alert list.

Generate insights only from actual findings and decisions belonging to the selected organisation and brand.

Identify repeated issues such as:
- The same approved rule being violated across different drafts.
- Repeated mismatch with an approved product fact.
- Repeated terminology mistakes.
- Recurring unsupported claims.
- Repeated problems associated with a particular channel.
- Ambiguous or conflicting rules that repeatedly require human intervention.

For a prototype, consider an issue recurring when the same rule or clearly matching issue occurs in at least three findings across at least two distinct drafts in a configurable seven-day window.

Do not group all issues in a category into one recurring problem merely because they share a category.

Show the matching evidence, affected drafts, relevant rule and time period. Treat an alert as a prompt for investigation, not proof that an individual employee is at fault.

Track potentially unclear guidelines separately. Examples include repeated “needs verification” findings, conflicting approved rules or frequent reviewer overrides. Provide an actionable recommendation to the administrator to clarify or update the guideline.

Do not automatically rewrite approved rules or train a model from reviewer decisions without explicit controls and a review process.

